From 5555555555555555555555555555555555555555 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niravkumar L Rabara <niravkumarlaxmidas.rabara@altera.com>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 13:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arch/armv8: vgicv3: fix MPIDR-as-shift in
 vgic_int_ptarget_mask

vgic_int_ptarget_mask() returns the bitmap of physical CPUs that should
receive a VGIC_ROUTE IPI when an interrupt is routed.  For non-broadcast
SPIs the original code computed:

	return (uint8_t)(1U << interrupt->phys.route);

interrupt->phys.route is set by vgic_int_set_route() above to

	cpu_id_to_mpidr(tvcpu->phys_id) & MPIDR_AFF_MSK

i.e. it is the AArch64 MPIDR_EL1 affinity field of the target physical
CPU, NOT a CPU index.  On a multi-cluster topology (Aff1 != 0) the LSL
instruction silently truncates the shift count to its bottom 5 bits, so

	1U << 0x100 (= cluster 1, core 0)  ==  1U << 0  ==  pCPU 0

The IPI is then misrouted to pCPU 0, which on a heterogeneous AMP
deployment (e.g. Bao co-hosting Linux on the A76 cluster + FreeRTOS on
the A55 cluster of an Intel Agilex5e) is a CPU running a *different*
guest VM.  When that pCPU receives the VGIC_ROUTE message it looks up
the interrupt in its own VM's vgicd state, fails to find the owner
vCPU, and prints

	"received vgic3 msg target to another vcpu"

before silently dropping the IRQ.  The kick (SPI 52, the bao-rproc /
OpenAMP rpmsg notify line) is then never delivered to Linux even
though the firmware already wrote the NS_ANNOUNCE buffer + advanced
vring0.used.idx in shared memory.  Reproduces 100% of the time when
Linux's GIC driver pins the IRQ to a single A76 pCPU (IRM = 0,
GICD_IROUTER aff = 0x100 / 0x101).

Fix: invert the MPIDR -> phys_id mapping with a linear scan over
platform.cpu_num before forming the bitmask.  The scan is on the IPI
fan-out hot path only (per-interrupt route, not per-instruction) and
platform.cpu_num is small in practice (4 on Agilex5e); cost is well
below the cost of a single cpu_send_msg() that follows.

Same shift bug exists for the broadcast path's "exclude current pCPU"
mask -- (1U << cpu()->vcpu->phys_id) -- but that is fed an actual
phys_id (not an MPIDR) so the original arithmetic is correct there;
left untouched.

vgicv2.c is unaffected: GICv2 does not have a multi-affinity router
register and vgic_int_ptarget_mask there reads the GICD_ITARGETSR
target byte (already a CPU bitmap) directly.

Tested on Agilex5e SoCDK with the hps-amp project's
agilex5-linux+freertos config: after this patch /proc/interrupts
shows the b0000000.bao-rproc IRQ counter incrementing on every kick
and /dev/rpmsg0 appears as soon as the peer firmware sends NS_ANNOUNCE.

Signed-off-by: Niravkumar L Rabara <niravkumarlaxmidas.rabara@altera.com>
---
--- a/src/arch/armv8/vgicv3.c	2026-05-23 13:44:45.941261128 +0800
+++ b/src/arch/armv8/vgicv3.c	2026-05-23 13:44:59.133261625 +0800
@@ -35,7 +35,29 @@
     if (vgic_broadcast(vcpu, interrupt)) {
         return (uint8_t)(cpu()->vcpu->vm->cpus & ~(1U << cpu()->vcpu->phys_id));
     } else {
-        return (uint8_t)(1U << interrupt->phys.route);
+        /*
+         * interrupt->phys.route is the AArch64 MPIDR (Aff[3:0]) of the
+         * target physical CPU, set by vgic_int_set_route() via
+         * cpu_id_to_mpidr(tvcpu->phys_id) & MPIDR_AFF_MSK.  Feeding it
+         * straight into "1U << ..." is wrong: AArch64 LSL truncates the
+         * shift count to its bottom 5 bits, so an MPIDR like 0x100
+         * (cluster 1, core 0) collapses to (1U << 0) = pCPU 0 -- on a
+         * heterogeneous AMP deployment that is a CPU running a
+         * different guest VM, and the VGIC_ROUTE IPI is silently
+         * misrouted (target prints "received vgic3 msg target to
+         * another vcpu" and drops the IRQ).
+         *
+         * Map MPIDR back to a phys_id with a linear scan; the scan is
+         * only on the IPI fan-out hot path and platform.cpu_num is
+         * small in practice (<= 8 on every supported SoC).
+         */
+        for (cpuid_t pcpu = 0; pcpu < platform.cpu_num; pcpu++) {
+            if ((cpu_id_to_mpidr(pcpu) & MPIDR_AFF_MSK) ==
+                (uint64_t)interrupt->phys.route) {
+                return (uint8_t)(1U << pcpu);
+            }
+        }
+        return 0;
     }
 }
 
